"Almost color-balanced perfect matchings in color-balanced complete graphs (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012365X21004143)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; # Almost color-balanced perfect matchings in color-balanced complete graphs ... While not every k-edge colored color-balanced complete graph {K}_{2kn}${K}_{2kn}$ has a color-balanced perfect matching M, that is, a perfect matching with $f(M)=0$, we prove the existence of a perfect matching M with $f(M)=O\\left(k\\sqrt{kn\\mathrm{ln}(k)}\\right)$ for general k and $f(M)\\le 2$ for $k=3$; the case $k=2$ has already been studied earlier.\n\n## Discrete Mathematics\n\nDate: February 2022\n\nArticle: 112701\n\nVolume: Volume 345, Issue 2\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n### Published by\n\nShow more\n\nResearch article\n\nOpen archive\n\nUnder an Elsevier user license\n\nGet rights and content\n\n# Almost color-balanced perfect matchings in color-balanced complete graphs\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Johannes Pardey, Dieter Rautenbach\n\nShow more\n\nView PDFDownload full issue\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2021.112701\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article\n  * Recommended articles\n  * Cited by (1)\n  * Metrics\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor a graph G and a not necessarily proper k-edge coloring $c:E(G)\\to \\{1,\\dots ,k\\}$, let ${m}_{i}(G)$ be the number of edges of G of color i, and call G color-balanced if ${m}_{i}(G)={m}_{j}(G)$ for every two colors i and j. Several famous open problems relate to this notion; Ryser's conjecture on transversals in latin squares, for instance, is equivalent to the statement that every properly n-edge colored complete bipartite graph {K}_{n,n}${K}_{n,n}$ has a color-balanced perfect matching. We contribute some results on the question posed by Kittipassorn and Sinsap (arXiv:2011.00862v1) whether every k-edge colored color-balanced complete graph {K}_{2kn}${K}_{2kn}$ has a color-balanced perfect matching M. For a perfect matching M of {K}_{2kn}${K}_{2kn}$, a natural measure for the total deviation of M from being color-balanced is $f(M)=\\sum _{i=1}^{k}|{m}_{i}(M)-n|$. While not every k-edge colored color-balanced complete graph {K}_{2kn}${K}_{2kn}$ has a color-balanced perfect matching M, that is, a perfect matching with $f(M)=0$, we prove the existence of a perfect matching M with $f(M)=O\\left(k\\sqrt{kn\\mathrm{ln}(k)}\\right)$ for general k and $f(M)\\le 2$ for $k=3$; the case $k=2$ has already been studied earlier. An attractive feature of the problem is that it naturally invites the combination of a combinatorial approach based on counting and local exchange arguments with probabilistic and geometric arguments.\n\n## Keywords\n\nZero-sum Ramsey theory\n\n;\n\nRainbow matching\n\n  * Previous article in this issue\n  * Next article in this issue\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nAs a special question from the area of zero-sum Ramsey theory [3], [8], Caro, Hansberg, Lauri, and Zarb [4] asked whether every 2-edge-colored complete graph of order 4n with equally many edges of both colors always has a perfect matching that also contains equally many edges of both colors. This question was answered independently and affirmatively by Ehard, Mohr, and Rautenbach [5] and by Kittipassorn and Sinsap [11]. The motivation for the present paper is an interesting problem formulated by Kittipassorn and Sinsap concerning possible generalizations of this result to settings with more than two colors. In particular, they [11] asked whether, for every k-edge-coloring of {K}_{2kn}${K}_{2kn}$ with equally many edges of each color, there is a perfect matching M that also contains equally many edges of each color; in other words, whether there is a color-balanced perfect matching for every color-balanced edge-coloring of {K}_{2kn}${K}_{2kn}$. Note that the considered edge-colorings are not required to be proper, and that an obvious necessary condition for the existence of M is that the order of the complete graph is a multiple of 2k. While the example in Fig. 1 shows that there is not always such a matching, we believe that there are always perfect matchings that are very close to being color-balanced.\n\nImage: Fig. 1\n\n  1. Download: Download high-res image (22KB)\n  2. Download: Download full-size image\n\n## 2. Proof of Theorem 2\n\nThroughout this section, let n and k be positive integers, and let $c:E({K}_{2kn})\\to [k]$ be such that for every i in $[k]$, there are equally many edges e with $c(e)=i$. Let $V({K}_{2kn})=[2kn]$. For i in $[k]$ and a (not necessarily perfect) matching M of {K}_{2kn}${K}_{2kn}$, let ${m}_{i}(M)$ be the number of edges e in M with $c(e)=i$.\n\nWe consider the following random procedure constructing a perfect matching of {K}_{2kn}${K}_{2kn}$:\n\nImage: Algorithm 1\n\n  1. Download: Download high-res image (28KB)\n  2. Download: Download full-size image\n\nAlgorithm 1. RPM.\n\nLet M be a random matching returned by RPM. Our goal is to use Azuma's inequality [1] (cf. [13], p. 92) in order to show that the random variable ${m}_{i}(M)$ is strongly concentrated. Therefore, let $({e}_{1},\\dots ,{e}_{j-1},{e}_{j})$ and $({e}_{1},\\dots ,{e}_{j-1},{e}_{j}^{\\prime })$ be two possible sequences of the first j edges xy selected by RPM for some j. Let K be the complete graph that arises by removing from {K}_{2kn}${K}_{2kn}$ the $2(j-1)$ vertices incident with the edges ${e}_{1},\\dots ,{e}_{j-1}$. By the selection of x within RPM, we have ${e}_{j}=xy$ and ${e}_{j}^{\\prime }=x{y}^{\\prime }$ for distinct vertices x, y, and {y}^{\\prime }${y}^{\\prime }$ of K. Let {\\mathcal{M}}_{j}${\\mathcal{M}}_{j}$ be the set of all perfect matchings of K that contain {e}_{j}${e}_{j}$, and let {\\mathcal{M}}_{j}^{\\prime }${\\mathcal{M}}_{j}^{\\prime }$ be the set of all perfect matchings of K that contain {e}_{j}^{\\prime }${e}_{j}^{\\prime }$. For {M}_{j}${M}_{j}$ in {\\mathcal{M}}_{j}${\\mathcal{M}}_{j}$, let the vertex {x}^{\\prime }${x}^{\\prime }$ be such that ${x}^{\\prime }{y}^{\\prime }\\in {M}_{j}$, and let$g({M}_{j})=({M}_{j}\\setminus \\{xy,{x}^{\\prime }{y}^{\\prime }\\})\\cup \\{x{y}^{\\prime },{x}^{\\prime }y\\}.$ Clearly, $g({M}_{j})\\in {\\mathcal{M}}_{j}^{\\prime }$. Note that, given the perfect matching $g({M}_{j})$ and knowing the fixed vertices x, y, and {y}^{\\prime }${y}^{\\prime }$, the vertex {x}^{\\prime }${x}^{\\prime }$ is uniquely determined as the matching partner of y in $g({M}_{j})$, that is, the original perfect matching {M}_{j}${M}_{j}$ can be reconstructed, which means that the function $g:{\\mathcal{M}}_{j}\\to {\\mathcal{M}}_{j}^{\\prime }$ is injective. Since $|{\\mathcal{M}}_{j}|=|{\\mathcal{M}}_{j}^{\\prime }|$, it follows that g is actually bijective.\n\nTrivially,$|{m}_{i}(M)-{m}_{i}(g(M))|\\le 2\\text{ for every }M\\text{ in }{\\mathcal{M}}_{j}\\text{ and every }i\\text{ in }[k]\\text{.}$ A simple bijective argument implies that, conditioned on the choice of ${e}_{1},\\dots ,{e}_{j-1},{e}_{j}$, the procedure RPM produces each extension {M}_{j}${M}_{j}$ of $\\{{e}_{j}\\}$ to a perfect matching of the complete graph K equally likely. For every i in $[k]$, this implies$\\left|\\mathbb{E}\\right[{m}_{i}(M)|({e}_{1},\\dots ,{e}_{j-1},{e}_{j})]-\\mathbb{E}[{m}_{i}(M)|({e}_{1},\\dots ,{e}_{j-1},{e}_{j}^{\\prime })\\left]\\right|=|\\frac{1}{|{\\mathcal{M}}_{j}|}\\sum _{{M}_{j}\\in {\\mathcal{M}}_{j}}{m}_{i}({M}_{j})-\\frac{1}{|{\\mathcal{M}}_{j}^{\\prime }|}\\sum _{{M}_{j}^{\\prime }\\in {\\mathcal{M}}_{j}^{\\prime }}{m}_{i}({M}_{j}^{\\prime })|=\\left|\\frac{1}{|{\\mathcal{M}}_{j}|}\\sum _{{M}_{j}\\in {\\mathcal{M}}_{j}}\\right({m}_{i}({M}_{j})-{m}_{i}(g({M}_{j}))\\left)\\right|\\le \\frac{1}{|{\\mathcal{M}}_{j}|}\\sum _{{M}_{j}\\in {\\mathcal{M}}_{j}}|{m}_{i}({M}_{j})-{m}_{i}(g({M}_{j}))|\\le 2.$ Since RPM returns each perfect matching of {K}_{2kn}${K}_{2kn}$ equally likely, we have $\\mathbb{E}[{m}_{i}(M)]=n$ (cf. Lemma 5 below), and Azuma's inequality implies$\\mathbb{P}[|{m}_{i}(M)-n|>t]\\le 2{e}^{-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8kn}}\\text{ for every }t>0\\text{ and every }i\\text{ in }[k]\\text{.}$ For $t=3\\sqrt{kn\\mathrm{ln}(2k)}$, the right hand side of the previous inequality is less than \\frac{1}{k}$\\frac{1}{k}$. Now, the union bound shows that, with positive probability,$f(M)=\\sum _{i=1}^{k}|{m}_{i}(M)-n|\\le 3k\\sqrt{kn\\mathrm{ln}(2k)},$ which completes the proof. □\n\n## 3. Proof of Theorem 3\n\nThroughout this section, let n be a positive integer, and let $c:E({K}_{6n})\\to \\{\\mathrm{red},\\mathrm{green},\\mathrm{blue}\\}$ be such that there are equally many red, green, and blue edges, that is, there are exactly $\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\begin{array}{c}6n\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)$ edges of each color. For a perfect matching M of {K}_{6n}${K}_{6n}$, let${r}_{M}=|\\{e\\in M:c(e)=\\text{ red}\\}|{g}_{M}=|\\{e\\in M:c(e)=\\text{ green}\\}|{b}_{M}=|\\{e\\in M:c(e)=\\text{ blue}\\}|,\\text{ which implies}\\phantom{\\rule{0ex}{0ex}}f(M)=|{r}_{M}-n|+|{g}_{M}-n|+|{b}_{M}-n|.$ For two sets N and {N}^{\\prime }${N}^{\\prime }$ of edges of {K}_{6n}${K}_{6n}$, let $E[N,{N}^{\\prime }]$ be the set of edges uv of {K}_{6n}${K}_{6n}$ such that u is incident with an edge in N and v is incident with an edge in {N}^{\\prime }${N}^{\\prime }$, and let $E[N]$ denote $E[N,N]$. Note that this definition implies $N\\subseteq E[N]$.\n\nNow, let the perfect matching M of {K}_{6n}${K}_{6n}$ be chosen such that $f(M)$ is as small as possible. In order to complete the proof, we need to show that $f(M)\\le 2$. While the claims below are formulated in terms of M, for our arguments it is important that the stated properties actually hold for all perfect matchings {M}^{\\prime }${M}^{\\prime }$ of {K}_{6n}${K}_{6n}$ with $f({M}^{\\prime })=f(M)$.\n\nLet${M}_{r}=\\{e\\in M:c(e)=\\text{ red}\\},{M}_{g}=\\{e\\in M:c(e)=\\text{ green}\\},{M}_{b}=\\{e\\in M:c(e)=\\text{ blue}\\},r={r}_{M}=|{M}_{r}|,\\phantom{\\rule{0.2em}{0ex}}\\phantom{\\rule{0.2em}{0ex}}\\phantom{\\rule{0.2em}{0ex}}\\phantom{\\rule{0.2em}{0ex}}g={g}_{M}=|{M}_{g}|,\\phantom{\\rule{0.2em}{0ex}}\\phantom{\\rule{0.2em}{0ex}}\\phantom{\\rule{0.2em}{0ex}}\\phantom{\\rule{0.2em}{0ex}}\\text{ and }b={b}_{M}=|{M}_{b}|.$ By symmetry, we may assume$r\\ge g\\ge b.$ If uv and xy are two edges in M, then we say that the two edges ux and vy are parallel; note that the two edges uy and vx are also parallel. If replacing uv and xy within M by the parallel edges ux and vy yields a perfect matching {M}^{\\prime }${M}^{\\prime }$ with $f({M}^{\\prime })<f(M)$, then we obtain a contradiction to the choice of M; in this case, we say that $\\{uv,xy\\}\\to \\{ux,vy\\}$ is a contradicting swap.\n\nClaim 1\n\n$|n-g|\\le 1$.\n\nProof of Claim 1.\n\nSuppose, for a contradiction, that $|n-g|\\ge 2$.\n\nFirst, we assume that $g\\ge n+2$. Note that $r\\ge n+2$ and $b\\le n-4$. If $uv,xy\\in {M}_{r}\\cup {M}_{g}$ and ux is blue, then the swap $\\{uv,xy\\}\\to \\{ux,vy\\}$ is contradicting. In fact, if {M}^{\\prime }${M}^{\\prime }$ denotes the perfect matching generated by the considered swap, then ${r}_{{M}^{\\prime }},{g}_{{M}^{\\prime }}\\ge n$, ${r}_{{M}^{\\prime }}+{g}_{{M}^{\\prime }}\\le r+g-1$, and $n\\ge {b}_{{M}^{\\prime }}\\ge b+1$, which implies $f({M}^{\\prime })<f(M)$. Hence, no edge in $E[{M}_{r}\\cup {M}_{g}]$ is blue. Similarly, if $uv\\in {M}_{b}$, $xy\\in {M}_{r}\\cup {M}_{g}$, and ux as well as vy are blue, then the swap $\\{uv,xy\\}\\to \\{ux,vy\\}$ is contradicting. This implies that at most half the edges in $E[{M}_{b},{M}_{r}\\cup {M}_{g}]$ are blue; more precisely, from every two parallel edges in $E[{M}_{b},{M}_{r}\\cup {M}_{g}]$ at most one is blue. These two observations imply that the total number of blue edges is at most(1)\\left(\\begin{array}{c}2b\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}2b(6n-2b)=(6n-1)b\\stackrel{b<n}{<}(6n-1)n=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\begin{array}{c}6n\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right),$\\left(\\begin{array}{c}2b\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}2b(6n-2b)=(6n-1)b\\stackrel{b<n}{<}(6n-1)n=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\begin{array}{c}6n\\\\ 2\\end{array}\\right),$ where the inequality uses the strict monotonicity of the binomial coefficient as well as of the function $b\\mapsto 2b(6n-2b)$. This contradicts the hypothesis that exactly one third of all edges are blue.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNonexistence of almost-complete Harary polynomials — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/372265/nonexistence-of-almost-complete-harary-polynomials)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; There is no Harary polynomial which is almost complete for $\\mathbb{G}(n,1/2)$G(n,1/2). ... Makowsky, “Distinctive power and comparability of Harary polynomial”, arXiv:2512.22556 (2025).\n\nNonexistence of almost-complete Harary polynomials\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet $\\mathbb{G}(n,1/2)$G(n,1/2) be the random graph model with edge probability $1/2$1/2, and let a Harary polynomial be a graph polynomial arising from a graph property. A graph polynomial is almost complete when asymptotically almost all graphs in the model are uniquely determined by it.\n\nHarary almost-completeness conjecture. There is no Harary polynomial which is almost complete for $\\mathbb{G}(n,1/2)$G(n,1/2).\n\nThis is proposed in contrast with the Bollobás–Pebody–Riordan conjecture for the chromatic polynomial. The supplied text gives no resolution or supporting theorem for this stronger nonexistence claim.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Johann A. Makowsky, “Distinctive power and comparability of Harary polynomial”, arXiv:2512.22556 (2025).\n\nchromatic-polynomialgraph-polynomialsrandom-graphs\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 2 days ago\n\n* * *--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMeta-theorems for Graph Polynomials (https://www.matr