g\n\non D. Instead of almost complete we can also consider the case where a certain\n\nproportion of graphs is F-unique on D.\n\nMore precisely, we look at the following: Let F be a graph polynomial, D be a\n\ngraph property. D(n) is the family of graphs in D of order n with vertices labeled\n\n1, ...n.U D\n\nF(n) is the family of F-unique graphs in D of order n.\n\nDeﬁnition 3.8.\n\n(i) A graph polynomial F is α-distinguishing on D if\n\nlim\n\nn→∞\n\n|U D\n\nF(n)|\n\nD(n)|=α.\n\n3 In [44] the independence polynomial is called the stable set polynomial and denoted by S(G;x).\n\n10 JOHANN A. MAKOWSKY\n\n(ii) A graph polynomial F is weakly distinguishing on D if α= 0.\n\n(iii) F is almost complete on D if α= 1. The number α measures the distinguishing\n\npower of F on D.\n\nWe call α the distinguishability index of F on D.\n\nFor G(n, p(n)) the Distinguishability index can be deﬁned similarly, but we do\n\nnot need this in the sequel of the paper.\n\nQuestion 5. \na circle C r.\n\n20 JOHANN A. MAKOWSKY\n\nProof. As b is a bridge, there are no edges in G between G 1 and G 2. Choose\n\nu′∈V(G 1), u′=u and v′∈V(G 2), v′=v. Then adding b′= (u′, v′) to G cannot\n\ngenerate a circle C r.□\n\nProposition 8.11. Every graph G∈ ∗\n\nr has a F r-mate.\n\nProof. Without loss of generality (Proposition 8.2(ii)) we can assume hat G is\n\nconnected.\n\nAssume G is at least 2-edge-connected. There are d, e two edges of G such that\n\nneither d nor e are part of a triangle. By Fact 4 G−d and G−e are χ∆-mates.\n\nIf G has a bridge b= (u, v), we can apply Fact 4 and choose b′= (u′, v′). Let G′be\n\nobtained from G by adding b′to G. We conclude that G and G′are F r-mates. □\n\nProof of Theorem 8.5(i).\n\n(i): We ﬁrst note that F orb(C(r, 1)) = r∪ ∗\n\nr∪ Q∗.\n\nThe graphs in r have maximum degree D= 2. By Proposition 8.8, almost all\n\ngraphs in G(n, d/n) have maximum degree ≥3. Hence almost all graphs are not\n\nin r.\n\nBy Proposition 8.11 all graphs in ∗\n\nr have a F r-mate.